    "result_text": "The full test suite passes: **276 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the cp12 rule (\"Title-case the city when creating an owner and return it in the 'city' field, but if an owner already exists in that city, reuse that existing owner's exact spelling instead\") using the project's OfficeFloor function-injection pattern.\n\n**New function** \u2014 `src/main/java/.../rest/function/owner/NormalizeCity.java`:\n- Title-cases the city (each whitespace-separated word capitalized, the rest lower-cased).\n- Before applying that, scans existing owners; if one already has a city matching case- and whitespace-insensitively, it reuses that owner's exact spelling instead. This mirrors the normalization conventions already used in `EnsureOwnerUnique`.\n\n**Pipeline wiring** \u2014 `src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`:\n- Inserted a `normalizeCity` step (under `govern: [ transaction ]`) between `assignMembership` and `assignCustomerCode`, so the per-city customer code (cp10) is counted against a single consistent city spelling.\n\nThe `@Val Owner` is mutated in place, so `AssignCustomerCode`, `SaveOwner`, and `RespondWithOwnerCreated` all see the normalized city \u2014 and `GET /api/owners/{id}` returns it. Both new cp12 tests and the existing cp10 city/customer-code tests pass, along with the entire suite.",
